## Sprint: Via Array Generation - Multi-Layer Stack Arrays
##
## This test verifies CONSTRAINT-DRIVEN via stack arrays.
## Deep layer transitions on power nets automatically get via arrays.
##
## The Declarative Truth:
## 1. User declares: VDD: power (net classification)
## 2. User declares: min_spacing: 2mm (spacing constraint)
## 3. User creates: z:1 to z:5 transition (4-layer span)
## 4. Compiler derives: "Power net + spacing + deep stack → via stack arrays"
##
## Expected Result:
## ✅ Via stack arrays for z:1→2, z:2→3, z:3→4, z:4→5
## ✅ Each layer has grid of vias
## ✅ All vias vertically aligned

import * from @std/primitives/units
import Copper from @std/materials/conductors

## Profile with via spacing (no "array" or "stack" keywords)
profile PowerStackArray:
    description: "Power distribution with via spacing"
    
    trace:
        min_width: 200µm
        min_spacing: 200µm
    
    via:
        min_diameter: 300µm
        min_enclosure: 150µm
        min_spacing: 2mm            # This + power net + deep stack = automatic arrays

module ViaStackArrayTest:
    pins: []

space TestViaStackArray implements ViaStackArrayTest:
    dimensions: 20mm by 20mm by 1mm
    grid: 200 by 200 by 10
    origin: tl by t
    profile: PowerStackArray
    
    nets:
        VDD: power      # Power net → via stack arrays
    
    ## Bottom VDD plane (z:1)
    add pour(Copper) named VDD_Bottom on z:1:
        boundary: [x: 5mm, y: 5mm] to [x: 15mm, y: 15mm]
        net: VDD
    
    ## Top VDD plane (z:5) - 4 layer span
    add pour(Copper) named VDD_Top on z:5:
        boundary: [x: 5mm, y: 5mm] to [x: 15mm, y: 15mm]
        net: VDD

## Expected behavior (derived from constraints):
## - 10mm x 10mm overlap
## - 2mm spacing → ~5x5 = 25 vias per layer
## - 4 layer transitions → 25 × 4 = 100 total vias
## - All vertically aligned in grid
## - Zero mode strings. Pure constraint-driven physics.
